WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.510
 Delicious Revolution is brought to you by us, by me and Chelsea, specifically by our consulting business.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:11.430
 We provide participatory research, communications, and creative work to organizations that are building food movements.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.370
 What's your food movement? Let us know and get in touch.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.670
 DeliciousRevolutionShow.com

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:26.130
 How plants and people and bodies are resilient is just mind-blowing.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:32.800
 Because I think the story of this food is as much the story of the people that eat it, that grow it, that transport it,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.190
 that have traveled with it, that have snuck it across borders, that have put seeds in their pockets.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.270
 I mean, that's the food that we eat in America today.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:45.710
 It was like, the majority of it was snuck into people's luggage on the way over from a very long journey.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.750
 One of the reasons I'm really interested in food and this date of 1492 of this, like, exploration,

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:57.310
 quote-unquote, into the new world and this moment where these staple ingredients that we all know and love,

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:02.640
 tomatoes, chilies, chocolate, all of that was taken from the Americas and is now a staple

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.860
 throughout the rest of the world and it ties into me to this question of gentrification that's happening

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.790
 in our neighborhoods now and the erasure of the histories of those ingredients.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:18.630
 I think what gentrification does and what colonialism does is it erases memory.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:24.690
 I think that part of that erasure, so much is lost, so much is lost.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:32.890
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.170
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.960
 And I'm Devin Sampson.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.190
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of food,

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:46.210
 including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists, bringing you in-depth conversations with some amazing people.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:52.770
 On this third season of Delicious Revolution, we're bringing you stories and perspectives from the unseen places in food systems.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:02:02.290
 Going behind kitchen doors, into underground nests of native bees, under the waves, and to the far away origins of flavors we love, just to name a few.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:08.420
 I'm speaking with people who work with food in places we normally cannot see or don't notice.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.250
 It's a season of unseen stories of food.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.170
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.340
 Just search for Delicious Revolution wherever you get your podcasts, and you'll find us.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:26.860
 Our website is deliciousrevolutionshow.com, where we have pictures, links, and more information about all the people on our show.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:36.200
 Sita Kuratomi Baumik is an artist, writer, and educator who understands art as a strategy to connect personal and public histories.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.390
 Her research focuses on decolonizing the hierarchy of the senses and the impact of migration.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:48.940
 She was raised in Los Angeles, she's based in Oakland, and she is Indian and Japanese-Columbian-American.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:56.180
 She is a founding member of the People's Kitchen Collective in Oakland, California, along with Jocelyn Jackson and Saqib Kival.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.640
 Together, they produce community meals that narrate our migration.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:06.660
 The goal of the People's Kitchen is not only to fill our stomachs, but also to nourish our souls, feed our minds, and fuel a movement.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.740
 Here's Chelsea's interview with Sita Baumik.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:17.570
 I just saw your Beyonce thing for the list for YBCA, which, congratulations, that's awesome.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.530
 I don't totally know what it is.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.410
 Maybe let's start there.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:21.770
 You guys are on the YBCA.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:22.950
 What's the list?

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:32.380
 So the YBCA 100 list is actually a list that's put together by the folks at YBCA of people that are influencing their work.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:46.300
 And so it's pretty incredible to be on this list with Steph Curry and Beyonce and then other local folks like Jeff Chang and Hank Willis Thomas and other artists that we also look up to.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.680
 But yeah, we found out actually from Saqib's roommate.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.340
 She came in and said, congratulations, guys.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:54.940
 And we said, cool, what for?

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.130
 It was like such an amazing surprise.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:07.170
 It was kind of awesome to be on the list with a bunch of other artists, but a bunch of just creative visionaries or whatever.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:17.660
 I really like how YBCA is pushing kind of the boundaries of what that means to make creative impact in communities, which I think is totally part of what you guys do, right?

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:18.830
 Hopefully.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:20.170
 Thanks.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:21.670
 Yes, absolutely.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:29.250
 I mean, I think that I guess in academic speak, you'd call it interdisciplinary, but I think in real life, that's just how we all work.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:39.240
 It's like we absorb so much from so many places and it's never just about food or about art or about any one particular thing.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:41.120
 Yeah, totally.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:42.620
 So welcome to the show, Sita.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.020
 I was just testing my levels, but then we got talking.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:52.170
 I'm here today with Sita Baumick, who's part of the People's Kitchen Collective, amongst other things.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:58.660
 And we're going to talk about some food traditions and all the projects they've been doing.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.820
 I went to this incredible dinner last weekend at Elena Studios, which is new, right?

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.160
 It's just recently opened.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.920
 And you should totally check out because there's going to be more things happening there.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.540
 So I guess when I think about you, I thought about like what started your love of food?

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:24.580
 So my love of food, it runs deep.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:31.160
 And it was something that I think was so, so present in my life growing up that I never actually paid attention to it.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.450
 Like that's how that's how deep it was.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:36.390
 And I my dad is the fruit whisperer.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:43.590
 He is so obsessed with growing fruit, eating fruit, finding fruit and vegetables.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:55.870
 And just growing up with that incredible care and love and excitement, like it just seemed like his face, it looked like he won the lottery when you find a new mango tree or plant something.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:06.550
 And I think that also for me, food is one of the ways that we communicate in our families and in terrible times when we have nothing to say to each other is the second that everybody gets together in the kitchen.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:07.350
 We all know our role.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:14.300
 Like we know who's supposed to get the napkins and we know who's supposed to finish the salad and who's supposed to sit where even.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:20.210
 And that I think has actually really been this huge source of communication for us.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:25.110
 And that's just the way of being with each other through wonderful things and through terrible things.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:26.310
 That's amazing.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:27.870
 So you grew up in L.A.

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:36.410
 And from what I can tell, your family, like it's it's a complicated place to be in Sita's family.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:37.930
 Like, tell me about your family.

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:40.450
 Tell me tell me what what it's like to be in your family.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.190
 So there's only four people in our immediate family.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:48.610
 It feels like 12, though, because each of us have a couple of personalities or something.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:54.530
 But my mother is Japanese Columbian and my father is Indian from West Bengal.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:02.190
 And, you know, we grew up I grew up in the 80s where actually being mixed in L.A.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:06.490
 was like really kind of normal and common, which is kind of incredible to think about now.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:16.950
 And and we yeah, I mean, we had this amazing kind of people would always ask me and still ask me, what did you eat growing up?

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:28.510
 And I'll be like, well, we'd have like lentils and miso soup and rice and keep going to that and fried plantains and, you know, and, and, and, and, and.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:33.840
 But yeah, my my folks, you know, a real little kind of outpost.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.140
 We didn't have a big family.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:40.300
 We have a huge extended family in other countries, but it was just the four of us.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.720
 I was just talking to another friend from L.A. who was talking about Seder.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.060
 And she was saying, I just thought that like matzo ball soup made with me.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:57.380
 So it was normal till I was like about 14 and I went somewhere else and realized that it was made with chicken broth because everybody's a vegetarian in her family.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:17.140
 And I don't know, I think about how so much of your work stems from people asking you questions, maybe like that, but that you just start telling people and then you there's this unraveling of like what the complexity of these stories of migration and history are in that.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:22.690
 How'd you come to kind of like, when did you realize that?

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:32.090
 And if it was normal to be mixed in L.A. in the 80s, like, I don't know, how did you start telling that story or something?

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:50.580
 So when I got to grad school in the MFA program at CCA, I had this moment where I decided to work with food partially because I knew that on some level it would be pleasurable for me if I decide if I if I use that kind of as a consistent material.

00:08:51.960 --> 00:09:03.930
 But also by that time, I'd really come to have a deep love for the way that ingredients hold stories and the stories of just these crazy migrations across oceans and across generations.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:22.620
 And I think one of the really amazing things about ingredients is that it even though seeds and ingredients and soil and all of these things change, it is this in some way you can you can taste the same flavors that your ancestors did and you can pass those on to the future.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:36.150
 And it's just this kind of like mashup of like past, present and future that has been circulating at the globe for, in most cases, about 500 years for a lot of ingredients when that that 1492 moment happened.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:40.010
 And I'm just fascinated by it.

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:45.290
 I tell my students that if you're obsessed with something, that the work will do itself.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:48.620
 And I feel like that's what ingredients do for me.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.400
 I'm so fascinated by it.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:10:01.120
 I am constantly asking questions and picking at things and putting things in my mouth and looking things up and finding out some incredible things about the way that the places that we've been in this world.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.280
 All right.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:09.330
 We're back from a short break because we had to eat some toast and talk about plums.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:13.290
 So tell me about plums in your family when you're done chewing.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.010
 This is a great interview.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:23.940
 Most cultures have this ingredient that's like kind of a mythical medicinal cure-all.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:35.800
 And for Japanese folks, I'm pretty confident about saying that that's umeboshi, which is this dried, salted, pickled plum that will basically cure everything and keep you alive forever.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:40.790
 And so with the People's Kitchen Collective, we started this project called the Kitchen Remedies.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:46.350
 And in the process of researching it, I was asking my mom just about different home remedies.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:51.150
 And she said, you know, when you had salmonella and you were younger, I asked your grandmother what to give you.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.170
 And she said, umeboshi.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:59.100
 And that's, I remember eating that when I was a kid with a little bit of rice just to settle my stomach.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:15.130
 And she also told me, and this is the part that I never heard before, that my grandmother was 18 when she left Japan to go to Colombia to get on a ship, to go to a place that she'd never been to, that's, you know, language that she'd never spoken before.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:19.330
 And she was in her 60s before she actually was able to go back for a visit.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:28.670
 And the first thing that she did was that she bought a barrel of umeboshi for every single family, Japanese American, Japanese Colombian family that lived in the Baida de Ngaoka in Colombia.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:41.620
 So my mother has this distinct memory of being 13 years old and the whole family getting this truck and going to the port, um, the, the port in Colombia and receiving these like giant barrels of plums.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:46.830
 And she said, you know, I said, the flavor is really strong.

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:49.070
 Like, did you, did you like it?

00:11:49.090 --> 00:12:00.170
 And she said, well, it never occurred to me not to like it because everybody was so excited that they were going to eat these plums that, you know, of course, like I just got caught up in it and I, I don't even know if I liked it or not.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:06.420
 And she started telling me that there were all these myths that the elders would tell them like, oh, if you eat it when you're well, it'll give you a stomachache.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.380
 But if you have a stomachache and you eat it, you'll get cured.

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:12.560
 And it was all these lies that they would tell the kids so that they wouldn't go in the basement and pick the barrels.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.660
 And so my mother left when she was 18 to come to the U.S.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:19.060
 And when she came back seven years later, they were still eating from those same barrels.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.600
 These plums last upwards of 10, sometimes 20 years.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:29.380
 They're considered not great eating after 10 years, but it was like so precious that in my family.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:29.900
 Yeah.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:31.980
 I mean, it was just edible gold.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.370
 And your dad loves plums, too, because he loves all fruit.

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:38.190
 Does he have a relationship with them?

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.730
 My dad doesn't have as special a relationship with plums as jackfruit.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:44.470
 This is his his new thing.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:46.690
 Every time I go to L.A., he buys me a new jackfruit.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:52.250
 And I don't know if you've ever seen these things, but it looks like if you dropped it off a forestry building, it could kill you.

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:58.310
 It's spiky and green and hard and it's sticky on the inside and kind of funky smelling.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:05.820
 The sweet thing about my dad is that anytime he thinks I like something, he'll go out and then, you know, buy tons of it.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:07.400
 And then I'll love it.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:10.120
 And then you can't even tell where the where it started.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:13.620
 You know, do I like it because he liked it or does he like it because I liked it?

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.880
 So now you're making umeboshi.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.300
 And where do these plums come from?

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:26.250
 The story of these plums is really fascinating because there were Japanese American families that were living in California.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:36.870
 It was basically a famine that had was the precedent for Japanese folks to spread throughout the Americas to Hawaii, California, Brazil, Peru, Colombia.

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:41.150
 And and in different waves of migration.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:45.250
 But these these families brought the seeds with them and planted them.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:52.310
 And after internment that happened in World War Two, the plums kind of the property.

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.450
 A lot of the Japanese American families lost their properties.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:58.390
 And so the plum trees are kind of scattered throughout the state.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:06.460
 And there are a few I've heard of one family in Northern California that's that's still growing and picking them.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.040
 But they're they're pretty difficult to come by.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:10.390
 That's interesting.

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:12.920
 Did does your family have one in L.A.?

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.330
 You know, they don't.

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:15.950
 My dad has papaya growing.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:20.950
 He's got all sorts of herbs, but no plums.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:22.470
 OK.

00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:22.970
 Yeah.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:25.290
 Well, so I lived in Japan when I was a baby.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:31.500
 And so I have a relationship with umeboshi too that, you know, it's for everything.

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:32.450
 Right.

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:42.770
 And what I didn't really realize, I mean, I'm just thinking about this as you're talking about this is I live somewhere now where Santa Rosa plums are the cultural lineage.

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:43.130
 Right.

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:44.870
 So they were grown mostly for prunes.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:49.370
 Actually, they were they were Italian plums and they were Santa Rosa plums and they were all ripped up for grapes.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.570
 But they were brought by the Italian families there.

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:59.860
 But everybody I mean, everyone has a big everybody does a lot of food preservation in Sonoma County.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:00.820
 And they don't.

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:04.490
 People are doing umeboshi with these other kinds of plums.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:06.770
 And I don't know if it's going to work or not.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:07.410
 Do you think it will?

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:10.100
 I've heard of some other experiments.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:17.740
 I mean, one of the other fascinating kind of lineages of these plums is that in Mexico, there's something called chamoy.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:22.220
 And chamoy is made with a chabacano, which is an apricot.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:26.960
 And it has there's a relationship between that and the umeboshi.

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:33.900
 And I think it's because of the unavailability of those green plums, apricots started to be substituted for that.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.340
 It's usually made with hibiscus and chili.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:41.640
 And it's like a savory, sweet kind of mildly spicy, like delicious sauce that you put on fruit.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:43.550
 Yeah.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.440
 Well, I didn't really realize that.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.280
 So that's like a Japanese lineage thing, you think.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:51.520
 Wow, that's interesting.

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.300
 So what's your connection to Mexico?

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.900
 My connection to Mexico is that we share a border with it.

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:06.440
 And I have a lot of friends and collectives and artisans that I've been working with over the years in Mexico.

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:09.480
 My husband's also Mexican-American, but his family all lives in the U.S.

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:10.560
 Cool.

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:15.490
 Tell me a little bit more about this project that you're doing about family cures.

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:19.040
 So how did that start and where is it now?

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:19.960
 Yeah.

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:26.700
 So the People's Kitchen Collective, which we're a collective of three chef, artists, activists based in Oakland, California.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.800
 It's myself and Saqib Kaval and Jocelyn Jackson.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:33.160
 And we do diaspora dinners.

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:37.160
 We do a free breakfast program in the spirit of the Black Panther Party for self-defense.

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:44.780
 And we work a lot with different institutions to talk about this idea of what food justice is and can be.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:51.380
 And as part of that, we had a questionnaire that we developed called...

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:53.040
 What actually was it called?

00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:53.740
 It's amazing.

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:57.380
 We had a questionnaire called A Recipe for Rebellion.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:05.570
 And it was asking people to kind of trace their own relationship to food and action and what that meant for them.

00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:17.630
 And one of the questions that kept standing out to me was, when you were a child and you were ill, what did your parents or caretakers or elders give you, feed you, or do for you to make you feel better?

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:20.750
 And that question just kept...

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:25.230
 It was like the question out of all of the words on that paper that just was so big to me.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:28.470
 Because everybody has had that experience.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:30.630
 Everybody's been sick at some point in their life.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:37.210
 And everyone, hopefully, has had someone come along and do something to help them feel better.

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:47.680
 And so we ended up turning that into an exhibition for this project called Crosslands at the Smithsonian in Washington, D.C.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:54.300
 And we made these prescriptions forms up and we asked hundreds of people the same question.

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:57.140
 You know, when you were sick as a child, what was it that made you feel better?

00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:04.770
 And the really interesting thing to me is, like, the responses are both so broad and there are so many overlaps.

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:18.300
 And the longer that folks, it seems, generationally were in the United States, to me, the more the response tended towards saltines and Coca-Cola.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:32.750
 And different foods that were more packaged, Campbell's chicken soup, that kind of thing, versus kind of things rooted in plants, more in plants, foods, teas, that kind of thing.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:34.720
 And it's really fascinating.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:39.560
 I mean, I'm, again, doing this project as an artist, not as somebody who's kind of crunching the data.

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:46.710
 But the response to one of the questions that we asked was also, what does this remedy remind you of?

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:49.350
 And I think so many people said it reminds me of home.

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:53.450
 And it's really also about the care as much as the cure.

00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:59.530
 Like, the cure is about the care that somebody gives you in saying, taking an hour out of their day to say, I'm worried about you.

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:00.210
 I care about you.

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:01.110
 Here's something.

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:03.450
 It almost, in some cases, doesn't matter what that thing is.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:20.690
 And in a lot of cases, what that was, we're finding out, you know, kind of backed by capital S Science, that there is totally huge and important and valid scientific reasoning behind why you would give somebody plums, for instance, when their stomach was upset.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:24.150
 What were those other foods that came up?

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:33.910
 So, one of the really incredible remedies that came up in different parts of the world was this remedy with an egg to remove fevers.

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:37.610
 And actually, not by eating the egg, but really kind of applying it to the body.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:49.610
 So, one person who's mong said that her mother would take a freshly hard-boiled egg and rub it on her body and break it open and bumps would appear on the outside of the egg yolk.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:53.450
 And she would repeat that over and over and over again until there were no bumps.

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:59.750
 And by the end of it, you wouldn't necessarily be healed completely, but you would have this intense relief and the fever would break.

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:10.920
 And we've heard of other remedies with putting an egg underneath a bed if you're sick to hold the fever or a raw egg being used to kind of hold the fever.

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:16.430
 But it's – and those we've heard from folks from Mexico.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:40.660
 And it's this – you know, I don't know much more about that than these stories that were shared, but I'm just starting to be really fascinated about, you know, how the egg can hold a fever, but also how it appears in two different places in the world and probably many more that are so far apart from each other.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:48.740
 Were there other things like that that people – I mean, I think about chicken soup, right?

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:49.600
 I think about some.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:21:01.410
 Yeah, we got a lot of Sprite, ginger ale, saltines, chicken soup, rice soups, lots of rice, lentils, turmeric.

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:11.130
 One of my favorite remedies was from Norma Listman, who collaborates with us, and it's from my grandfather, actually, in a recipe for heartache.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:17.350
 And so it's a cura para mal de amor, which is a remedy for heartbreak.

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:25.180
 And it's a rose, petals, honey, and mint put into a glass, and it has to be see-through.

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:37.840
 And the most important part of the remedy is that you hold – you cup the glass between your hands, and you put your love into it before giving the remedy to someone else or drinking it yourself.

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:46.040
 We actually had somebody try this, and she said that it was the first time in weeks that she'd been able to go to sleep without crying.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:53.990
 And, you know, her grandfather actually does this with all of his home remedies, regardless of what it is.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:22:02.940
 And it's this really beautiful intention that's set of kind of transferring that healing power into someone else.

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:16.370
 So we've actually heard a few remedies like that, but it's really wonderful for me to hear, like, the breadth of what healing means.

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:26.210
 You know, you could – because it's so confined to pills, I think, in the U.S. and for a lot of the kind of parts of our lives.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:32.550
 But to think about, like, oh, no, that there's so many other ways to think about healing and where it happens and how it happens.

00:22:32.650 --> 00:22:40.400
 Well, one thing I think about when you're talking is this idea of power, right, and where power lies and things.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:50.740
 So a lot of times when people talk about – well, I guess nutrition and starvation, right, or lack of nutrition or obesity, it's all about calories.

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.160
 And for me, somehow, that story always feels really flat.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:23:01.280
 And it seems like – I mean, it is really flat, right, like this soylent or whatever, you know.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:11.260
 Like, people just don't live healthy, balanced, beautiful, powerful, interesting-feeling lives eating just soylent, right?

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:13.660
 Like, food is so ingrained in what we do.

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:25.560
 But I think when – last Friday at this event, you guys were, you know, we're talking about decolonizing your bodies and decolonizing your minds and decolonizing your kitchens.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:30.870
 And there's this idea that power exists in food, and maybe it exists in so many different ways.

00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:34.600
 Is that part of the exploration or –?

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:48.020
 One of the reasons I'm really interested in food and this date of 1492 of this, like, exploration, quote-unquote, into the new world and this moment where these staple ingredients that we all know and love –

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:54.740
 tomatoes, chilies, chocolate – all of that was taken from the Americas and is now a staple throughout the rest of the world.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:24:08.230
 And it ties into me to this question of, like, gentrification that's happening in our neighborhoods now and the erasure of the histories of those ingredients.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:15.540
 I think what gentrification does and what colonialism does is it erases memory, and that's something that Saqib Kival, who I cook with, talks about a lot.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:22.400
 And I think that part of that erasure – so much is lost.

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:23.480
 So much is lost.

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:30.090
 And so I have this one story that I tell people of this moment where I was cooking with my grandmother in India.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:40.840
 And she – the first thing she does whenever she's cooking any kind of fish or – she doesn't actually cook meat, but anytime it's cooked in the household, she takes a handful of turmeric and she throws it on there.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:48.710
 And she does that, I realized, because they don't have refrigerators, and turmeric is antibacterial and it's antiseptic.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:59.110
 And so you're taking this thing and you're basically coating it and protecting it while you're going about, you know, doing the rest of your kitchen prep and then you go to cook it.

00:24:59.530 --> 00:25:04.070
 And I've seen that step taken out of so many recipes and it's, like, added in later.

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:10.360
 And that knowledge and that wisdom, it's, like, how do you know if you – if you don't know, you don't know.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:28.020
 And how do we preserve that knowledge and wisdom in a way that, like, if we lose that, that's a part of, you know, if we get stuck in the woods and we just happen to have a package of turmeric, we could keep ourselves alive a couple more days if we knew that little trick.

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:46.860
 But I think about – I think about some of these interviews that I did with people who were starving and who would talk about resiliency, like, in terms of, like, well, we don't really like yucca, but when there's nothing else, we dig it up and then we make tortillas out of yucca or whatever, you know.

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:50.930
 And I think that those two stories are related to each other, right?

00:25:50.990 --> 00:25:58.490
 There's these kind of invisible threads that pull through people's lives that are about resiliency and adaptation and change.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:07.110
 But they're so ordinary until they disappear that maybe they're not even seen sometimes.

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:10.910
 And I think we all do those things.

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:15.280
 Like, we all have our turmeric that we throw on our – that we throw on our meat.

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:24.120
 I guess I see your work as, like, there's this shining light on what the potency of those things are and that articulation of something like that.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:28.960
 Like, you have to hold this cure for a heartache with love.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:32.350
 Otherwise, it's just roses and – right?

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:36.980
 I mean, so it, like, changes from ordinary to extraordinary, like, with these steps.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:44.770
 And, yeah, I'm not sure what that question is exactly, but it feels important.

00:26:44.910 --> 00:26:54.940
 It feels like – it feels like that makes sense that you're an artist rather than a sociologist or rather than someone else there because there's some magic happening.

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:58.590
 There's some invisibility being made visible in that process.

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:03.450
 Does it get easier to see as you do it more?

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:12.000
 One of the reasons I'm drawn to the work that I do is because I have such a sense of wonder about it.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:24.890
 And I feel like I'm always just learning new things, and I think that any time that fart goes off, I'm like, wow, you know, that's – that's when I – I guess that's my version of seeing it.

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:27.550
 That's how I know I've seen something.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:29.340
 Does it happen all the time?

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:30.780
 That's a good question.

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:34.340
 It happens – it never ceases to amaze me.

00:27:34.340 --> 00:27:41.390
 Like, I – at this point, I should assume that, you know, every ingredient has an amazing story, and I guess I do believe that.

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:42.950
 But I am always blown away.

00:27:43.070 --> 00:27:50.110
 Like, I was just looking up yucca, and yucca is from indigenous to South America.

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:51.010
 It's not Brazil.

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:59.300
 And I found this out yesterday that it was basically taken by the Portuguese and then introduced into Africa, where it is an incredibly important staple crop.

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:04.460
 I mean, African food without yucca is like, what, you know?

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:11.320
 And it's, you know, it's a process that, again, took like 500 years, you know, which is no small amount of time.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:20.220
 But just how plants and people and bodies are resilient is just mind-blowing.

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:36.320
 Because I think the story of these – the story of this food is as much the story of the people that eat it, that grow it, that transport it, that have traveled with it, that have snuck it across borders, that have put seeds in their pockets.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:40.020
 I mean, that's the food that we eat in America today.

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:46.680
 It was like, majority of it was snuck into people's luggage on the way over from a very long journey.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:53.300
 And so where do you think this idea of – I don't know, I hate to use the word ownership because that's not exactly what I mean.

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.760
 But like, what does sovereignty look like in that?

00:28:55.940 --> 00:29:02.140
 And how do you tell – like, what's the narrative of who gets what and who gets to claim what within that space?

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:04.320
 Do you mean sovereignty like –

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:06.740
 Who – like peanuts.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.170
 Who claims that as theirs?

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:21.750
 And what happens when more than one group or more than one, I feel like, person or family, I feel like, in all these different scales, what happens when it's theirs and it's theirs and it's theirs?

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:25.510
 Is it then ours or is this a different story emerge?

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:35.580
 It's an interesting question in the face of a lot of articles that I've seen recently about this question of cultural appropriation through food and, you know, who – like, as you were saying, like, who gets to cook what?

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.980
 And who has the agency to cook that?

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:53.120
 But I think the difference between – there's a difference between appropriating and appropriating meaning erasing the history of that ingredient versus learning from that ingredient and using it along with all of its – all of its story.

00:29:53.500 --> 00:30:00.010
 It's a tricky line and I don't know that there are any kind of hard and fast rules around it.

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:13.330
 But I learn from so many other people about food traditions that are not mine and so many of those ingredients get kind of incorporated into my own diet and the way that I cook.

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:18.400
 I think that's another very American kind of way of being.

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:31.290
 But it's really about not losing the meaning and the geography and the story behind those ingredients and honoring that.

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:32.680
 How do you do that?

00:30:34.580 --> 00:30:52.400
 I mean, if one were just to think, like, to use food as an opportunity, like, as, you know, part of, like, your daily way of living to think about histories of colonization or liberation or just being in a complex world, like, how would you begin to do that?

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:56.420
 I think one of the most important things that people can do for themselves is cook their own food.

00:30:57.080 --> 00:31:04.070
 And I really do think that it's one of the things that's going to save us in this crazy capitalist system that we live in.

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:19.340
 And there are a lot of forces working against us to prevent us from actually doing that, to tell us that it's too hard, that it's easier to eat out, that it's easier to go get something on the street corner, or that all of your meals have to come from a restaurant or in a box of ingredients that gets delivered to your door.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:25.540
 And, you know, I support folks eating and nourishing themselves in all the ways that they find right for themselves.

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:30.440
 And there are some amazing restaurants, food businesses, projects that are doing incredible work.

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:45.120
 But I also think that it's really scary to me to think that we, that this thing that we need to do every single day has become this thing that's, for a lot of people, very kind of scary, almost.

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:46.880
 Like, oh, I don't know how to do that.

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:47.820
 I don't know what to do with it.

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:51.680
 And I think it starts there.

00:31:53.170 --> 00:32:04.290
 And that portal is like the kind of window into kind of knowing more about that deeper connection to things.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:11.300
 As an artist, like, I always say that the materials, like, let them, it's my biggest challenge to let the materials speak to me.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:15.310
 And, like, the tomato will tell you what the tomato wants it to be.

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:20.790
 Like, the tube of purple paint will tell you what the tube of purple paint wants it to be if you just give it enough time.

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:29.480
 And sometimes it literally means getting a bowl of tomatoes and having it next to you and staring at it or just being around it long enough for it to tell you something.

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:32.480
 But it will tell you all of those things.

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:34.740
 You'll have those exchanges with people at the grocery store.

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:45.380
 Or if you're growing it, you know, somebody will tell you this incredible story about how, you know, wherever, you know, they are from, this was done in a certain way.

00:32:45.380 --> 00:33:00.000
 And I think that that, like, that exchange, like, food exchange has so many hands by the time it, from the seed to your table, that the more you are involved in that arc, the more that you'll learn about that.

00:33:00.170 --> 00:33:05.510
 You kind of can't ignore it in a lot of ways because people will tell you.

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:09.090
 Well, so two things come up for me.

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:25.120
 One is that I want to hear you talk about care as a concept and how that exists in these complex things that were, that you're untangling through creating these meals and these experiences for people.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:28.840
 And then the other thing is visibility, right?

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:35.740
 So I just recently interviewed Saru Jayaraman and was thinking a lot about restaurants and restaurant workers.

00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:38.560
 And just like anything else, like, I'm very focused on farmers.

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:39.800
 I've always worked with farmers.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:44.640
 And it was so interesting to me to complexify, to complexify it, right?

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:54.340
 To get interested, to get curious, to sit with that a little bit and think about how those hands are touching my food and everybody else's food all the time, whether you notice it or not.

00:33:54.500 --> 00:33:57.720
 So how do you address those things?

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.140
 Maybe let's start with care first.

00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:04.530
 The idea of care goes back to this kitchen remedies project that we do as a People's Kitchen Collective.

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:14.100
 And I think also for my father, food was at times incredibly scarce.

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:24.430
 He lived through one famine himself and really, I think, knows the value of food and what it means to not have enough.

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:31.400
 And I think because of that, also, food became a way of reward and of celebration.

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:42.930
 And, you know, the stories of my grandmother picking the perfect banana and saving it just for him so he could have his one banana.

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:51.360
 You know, it's that care is so closely tied to it.

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:55.220
 I recently did a meal for a few friends at home.

00:34:55.300 --> 00:35:05.130
 And one of the things that I was thinking about was what was the – what moment in my life did I feel the most love and care through food?

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:09.020
 And for me, it's the care packages.

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:12.000
 It even says care in the name of it.

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:13.220
 I never thought about that before.

00:35:13.360 --> 00:35:19.720
 But these packages that my aunts in other countries would make for me to send me off to the airport.

00:35:20.760 --> 00:35:30.830
 And, like, I would take a box of candy or something, and so she would take it out of the box, and she would use that box to pack it full of hard-boiled eggs.

00:35:30.970 --> 00:35:31.850
 This is my aunt in India.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:42.170
 Hard-boiled eggs and fried bread and some, you know, probably a couple pieces of fruit or something and tie it all up with a string and send me off to the airport with it.

00:35:42.170 --> 00:35:54.190
 And just, like, the experience of just, like, unwrapping that on the airplane or in a bus terminal somewhere and just – it's, like, heartbreaking and heartwarming to me all at the same time every time it happens.

00:35:55.450 --> 00:36:11.710
 Because it's a sign that you're leaving someplace, but it's also this, like, this gesture of care that even though, you know, she and I may not share the same language, that I – it is so clear that both of us understand what that means.

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:16.220
 And, yeah, that's –

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:27.130
 That, I think, is what – how I know the power of food because I feel like I've held people's love in my hand.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:29.120
 It's totally visceral, right?

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:32.800
 I think to a fault I always say yes when people offer me food.

00:36:33.940 --> 00:36:36.800
 And I'm very offended when people say no to my food.

00:36:37.580 --> 00:36:44.120
 And I don't know how I would survive, actually, without that act of love all the time, right?

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:51.270
 And that draws me in deeper and deeper to food all the time because it's so tangible, right?

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:53.850
 It's, like, these actual creations of love.

00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:58.850
 And with all its imperfection and all of its – it reflects all the things around it.

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:05.670
 And, yeah, that makes me – I get kind of sappy thinking about that with all of those acts of care that way.

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:25.890
 And for me, that's a real reason to eat at home and to eat in other people's homes and to continue that is to have that visibility and to also have that vulnerability of, like, what it looks like for people to try to love you that way and to try to love other people that way.

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:34.160
 Okay, so I'm curious, like, when you guys make these big events for community with People's Kitchen Collective, I don't know what size they normally are.

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:35.920
 I don't know what they look like.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:42.560
 But you did a lot of talking at this event I went to in Oakland because you were invoking all these things.

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:45.230
 Is that a part of your work?

00:37:45.770 --> 00:37:46.110
 Yes.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:55.430
 We always, with our work, want to invoke both the struggle and the celebration of the ingredients and the people.

00:37:55.770 --> 00:38:06.670
 I think that we experience, specifically as folks of color, this reality that is, like, at once both really kind of painful and celebratory.

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:19.190
 And I think that that's certainly the way that, you know, I live my day, you know, thing – and I think many people do – is moments of incredible joy and pain that are sometimes simultaneously – simultaneous.

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:24.930
 And I think that that's one thing that there isn't a lot of space for in restaurants because it is about pleasure.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:32.100
 Food is so much about pleasure and about, like, having this, like – you know, we talk about food porn or, you know, food photos.

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:41.180
 And it's, like, everybody's so obsessed with getting the next delicious, expensive thing or cheap thing or whatever thing.

00:38:41.180 --> 00:38:43.200
 But it's about consuming, consuming, consuming, consuming, consuming.

00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:45.560
 And we do some funny things.

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:51.680
 Like, we don't publish menus before people come because we don't want it to be about, I get this.

00:38:51.940 --> 00:38:52.820
 I get that.

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:55.160
 It's, no, you're coming for the experience.

00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:58.380
 And we have to have a mutual trust about, we'll take care of you when you're here.

00:38:58.760 --> 00:39:00.590
 And we do invoke ancestors.

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:18.000
 It's actually one of the things that all three of us do that is probably not standard in most food businesses is that we call in our ancestors to guide us through the kitchen.

00:39:18.400 --> 00:39:20.120
 We sage the kitchen before we start.

00:39:20.480 --> 00:39:25.340
 We take photos of our ancestors with us to all of the events that we do.

00:39:25.760 --> 00:39:30.760
 We sometimes have the audience also call out the names of their own ancestors into the space.

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:32.420
 The food just tastes better when they're there.

00:39:32.660 --> 00:39:37.660
 It's just, you know, it's added insurance that everything's going to be all right.

00:39:38.810 --> 00:39:45.640
 And we do want to presence, again, that, like, migration of these foods.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:47.260
 So, yeah, we talk a bit.

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:51.270
 Can you talk about paradox in that?

00:39:51.400 --> 00:39:55.010
 So that celebration and that struggle that exists hand in hand?

00:39:55.010 --> 00:40:01.410
 I mean, so for my father, and actually, so my mother's Japanese Colombian.

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:14.080
 So there was, again, that famine that happened to my great, it was my great, sorry, my grandparents' generation that was really the catalyst for all of the migration out of Japan.

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:19.060
 And then also in India, my uncle survived through two famines, my father through one.

00:40:19.060 --> 00:40:29.210
 And I don't think it's an accident that the way that he celebrates is by cooking far more food than four people could ever eat.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:35.520
 And I think for me, that's kind of part of that paradox of that plenty and not enough.

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:38.900
 And it's really complicated.

00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:43.890
 But that's the way that he expresses his love and care.

00:40:44.030 --> 00:40:45.110
 So, you know, I'll take it.

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:50.290
 I'm curious about the role of gender, too, in this.

00:40:50.550 --> 00:41:01.680
 So I think something that felt so good to me on Friday night was this willingness to be with the complexity of these stories, right?

00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:07.060
 So that was kind of addressing race, and it was addressing class, and it was addressing history, and it was addressing gender.

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:15.320
 Like there were, that was a mixed group of people cooking and serving food in every way that, and so how do you address that in your process?

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:17.200
 And how do you talk about that?

00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:31.480
 Yeah, it's, it does actually, we do address it intentionally, because some of the things that feel sometimes kind of like, oh, you know, somebody is kind of more naturally inclined to do one thing or the other.

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:36.200
 Well, public perception of that is actually very different in terms of like, who is a quote, unquote, leader in the group.

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:39.240
 And we're really committed to not having a hierarchy.

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:50.370
 One of the best compliments that we have ever gotten was we have a young chef, Darius, who is in the under 10 crowd, that comes and loves cooking with us, actually.

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:55.590
 He comes out and volunteers with us, and his mother, Rose, who's an incredible chef as well.

00:41:55.830 --> 00:41:59.690
 And so he asked us, like, hey, who's in charge here?

00:42:00.770 --> 00:42:03.360
 And somebody said, well, who do you think is in charge?

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:07.140
 And he looked around the room, and he's like, I don't know, I can't tell.

00:42:07.900 --> 00:42:10.000
 He said, well, why do you need to know who's in charge?

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:13.640
 And he said, well, I want to get a job here, so I want to know who to ask for a job.

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:24.100
 And it was just like such a cool compliment to have this little young one, you know, ask us, like, one, who's in charge because I want to come back,

00:42:24.100 --> 00:42:33.440
 but recognizing that there are power structures in place and that we weren't also playing into that, that there wasn't a lead necessarily.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:41.030
 And the fact that he could see that was like a revalidation of like, yes, this is actually what we're trying to create.

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:48.590
 Commercial kitchens, the language around it and the positions are based on the French military.

00:42:49.390 --> 00:43:05.270
 And it's very patriarchal, and there's no lack of examples of how folks of color usually are back of house, and not only back of house, but, you know, washing dishes,

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:16.300
 I'm doing kind of the less glorified tasks in the kitchen and, you know, how that space includes women and particularly women of color.

00:43:17.590 --> 00:43:21.830
 It's the exact opposite by design of a home kitchen.

00:43:22.750 --> 00:43:38.970
 And I think what we try to do is really take the best of those to try to decolonize that space and make the food and conditions that don't replicate that system of oppression.

00:43:39.510 --> 00:43:47.490
 That's a complicated thing to do, which kind of leads me to like, I don't know why I'm asking for a lot of how-tos today.

00:43:47.910 --> 00:43:48.810
 It's kind of funny.

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:51.330
 But what does it mean to decolonize your kitchen?

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:53.770
 Like, how do you decolonize your home kitchen?

00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:58.250
 I mean, there's all this talk about local food.

00:43:58.630 --> 00:44:02.870
 There's like lots of places there are farmer's markets and lots of places there are no farmer's markets, right?

00:44:03.670 --> 00:44:08.230
 There's price points, there's all this kind of ability for access to food.

00:44:08.350 --> 00:44:11.790
 So, like, how do you start to decolonize your kitchen?

00:44:11.950 --> 00:44:18.180
 How is that a different way of thinking about this than stories that reify people's stories of oppression?

00:44:18.750 --> 00:44:26.570
 Or alternatively, all of this sort of like disgusting exotification of every part of food, right?

00:44:26.570 --> 00:44:35.230
 From the exquisiteness of dining to the, I guess, this kind of myth of perfection about what you could do with your body and your mind through food.

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:45.750
 There's an incredible resource, Decolonize Your Diet, by Luz and Catriona, who are both professors and have written this book about exactly that, decolonizing your diet.

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:54.260
 And what I love about their project is that they really take it down to, okay, let's talk about rice and beans, you know?

00:44:54.760 --> 00:45:05.850
 And I think that a lot of – there's a lot of foods that get classified as kind of poverty food, and sometimes people will be shamed for eating them, and then other times people will be glorified for eating them.

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:11.810
 Depending on the powers that be and, you know, what's selling on supermarket shelves and all those things.

00:45:12.070 --> 00:45:29.670
 But eating with your hands, the way you eat food, too, is one of those things that people have been shamed for all over the world for eating with your hands and, you know, not using metal between you and your food to mediate your experience.

00:45:29.750 --> 00:45:31.270
 Why would you want metal in between you and your food?

00:45:31.840 --> 00:45:32.480
 It's so terrible.

00:45:32.680 --> 00:45:32.940
 Cold.

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:41.210
 But, yeah, it's the way that we eat.

00:45:41.330 --> 00:45:42.410
 It's the things that we eat.

00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:47.250
 It's just – I think for me, decolonizing is about an awareness, about questioning.

00:45:47.750 --> 00:45:55.930
 It's about not just accepting something that you see on a store at face value and asking, like, no, but where did that come from?

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:57.250
 No, but really, where did that come from?

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:59.010
 No, but really, where actually did that come from?

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:01.050
 And what is its story?

00:46:01.290 --> 00:46:13.790
 And I'm thinking about just the complexity of home kitchen politics in this and how we were just talking about the pleasure of receiving love from – love and care from all of these people.

00:46:14.350 --> 00:46:20.090
 And that I guess ideally, for me, that starts farther back, right?

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:37.090
 That you get to participate in all parts of that process of love with the paradoxes that exist there and get to address them through cooking and eating and cleaning and all the parts that go along with food, right?

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:52.560
 That we get a choice with this kind of mindfulness of using this paradigm of decolonization to find moments of togetherness and ask questions where there are moments of apartness in it, right?

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:56.060
 I hear that through your stories with your family, that it's messy.

00:46:56.680 --> 00:46:58.490
 Always going to be messy.

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:00.680
 And that's the magic, too, right?

00:47:01.530 --> 00:47:01.710
 Yeah.

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:11.730
 I was talking to a friend of mine's mother, and she said that her hope, her wish for – one of her wishes for the future is that food is no longer used as a weapon.

00:47:12.840 --> 00:47:23.830
 And that happens on a huge scale, whether you're talking about access to food, the fact that – just insanity of the fact that we have more food in this world than there are people, and we can't get it to people to actually feed them.

00:47:24.530 --> 00:47:34.790
 But she was talking about it on a much more personal level in that her stepmother really had used food as a way of withholding from them as children and punishing them, you know, and reprimanding them.

00:47:35.270 --> 00:47:46.560
 And she, not so coincidentally, is an amazing cook now and really just one of the women that I learned how to cook from as an adult.

00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:54.560
 But it is – it's such a place of both harm and healing in so many ways.

00:47:55.290 --> 00:47:57.290
 So how do you teach people how to cook?

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:00.730
 You know, I don't – well, let's see.

00:48:00.770 --> 00:48:01.710
 How do I teach people how to cook?

00:48:02.510 --> 00:48:07.910
 I've talked a lot about feeding the masses, I guess, because we feed upwards of 500 people sometimes.

00:48:08.210 --> 00:48:12.250
 And I do a lot of community fundraisers and things like that.

00:48:13.060 --> 00:48:29.840
 And I actually love the kind of messy experience of just having, like, a crew of folks of just all different skill levels and just an eagerness and ages, too, and just coming with an eagerness to just get their hands in it.

00:48:31.760 --> 00:48:38.940
 And I think that it's so overwhelming to people to think about cooking for more than a couple people.

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:50.260
 That's the biggest hurdle, and I think if we can break it down into smaller, more managers – like, you know, 400 people is just 100 groups of four people.

00:48:50.980 --> 00:48:53.720
 For me, if I think about it that way, it's a lot less intimidating.

00:48:56.520 --> 00:49:02.200
 And that's one of the, you know, the ways that I talk to people about, like, oh, you're thinking about feeding 100 people?

00:49:02.300 --> 00:49:03.840
 Okay, we'll divide it into four.

00:49:03.960 --> 00:49:05.020
 That's only 25 people.

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:07.140
 That's about how many people you had at Potluck last week.

00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:07.760
 You're good.

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:08.760
 Just make four of those.

00:49:10.490 --> 00:49:12.020
 That's amazing.

00:49:12.260 --> 00:49:20.150
 So you use a lot of trying to figure out how people can contextualize it in their own lives with what they already know how to do.

00:49:21.110 --> 00:49:21.430
 Absolutely.

00:49:22.130 --> 00:49:24.430
 And, you know, cooking's not for everybody.

00:49:24.590 --> 00:49:25.390
 That's fine, too.

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:27.550
 Just, you know, buddy up with somebody who likes to do it.

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:28.550
 Maybe you can grow something.

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:29.210
 They can cook it.

00:49:29.330 --> 00:49:29.950
 I don't know, you know.

00:49:30.890 --> 00:49:32.830
 And dishwashing is a good thing, too.

00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:33.050
 Yeah, yeah.

00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:33.490
 You know, yeah.

00:49:33.790 --> 00:49:35.070
 Not underrated.

00:49:36.310 --> 00:49:40.670
 Well, maybe let's wrap it up by talking about, like, acts of imagination.

00:49:41.230 --> 00:49:56.600
 Because I think a lot of these things are these, they sound like experiments in organizing to me, but they are these kind of inherently utopic possibilities that then people get to go back to their lives and do whatever they want with that.

00:49:56.760 --> 00:49:59.890
 So, as an artist, what is that?

00:49:59.890 --> 00:50:00.730
 Why do you do that?

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:03.490
 And what do you hope from that, I guess?

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:23.760
 So, I really learned how to cook in quantity through a leftist football club, soccer club, like, not American football, which is a group that started in Oakland and now is in Oakland, L.A., the Twin Cities, Chicago, New York, and D.C.

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:35.440
 And it's a group of activists, educators, all sorts of community folks that just love football, football, sorry, soccer.

00:50:37.380 --> 00:50:45.340
 And I remember, you know, just there was a game and I said, you know, instead of going out, why don't you just come over and eat?

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:49.820
 And it was the first and last time that everybody fit around the table because that was eight people.

00:50:50.490 --> 00:50:56.180
 And after that, it was, like, 25, 40, 50 people and I just kept cooking and cooking and cooking.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:51:08.420
 And I think the reason that I kept cooking was because I saw that, you know, on the email list, there would be a spike in people who are sending out, like, job opportunities or, like, hey, I have a place to rent.

00:51:08.420 --> 00:51:15.780
 Or, you know, these things that are unrelated to food in a way are completely related to this experience of sharing and eating together.

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:18.640
 And I think that's what kept me in it.

00:51:18.720 --> 00:51:27.380
 It was just this fascinating kind of, like, oh, okay, like, if we do this and if we eat together, then this person can get a job.

00:51:27.500 --> 00:51:32.740
 And this person can, you know, feel like they, you know, have a new home even though they just moved here.

00:51:32.740 --> 00:51:51.620
 And that is, it's, that's, I think, the reason that, you know, I'm so drawn to these, like, huge events where they're, you're really building community and growing it.

00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:56.080
 Like, you're really just, like, kind of multiplying community every time that you share food.

00:51:58.300 --> 00:51:59.540
 That's pretty transformative.

00:52:00.540 --> 00:52:06.120
 How do we follow along with the People's Kitchen Collective and what you're up to next?

00:52:06.120 --> 00:52:08.460
 So, I have a website.

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:15.920
 It's my name, sitabamik.com, S as in Sam, I-T-A-B-H-A-U-M-I-K.com.

00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:23.070
 And the People's Kitchen Collective is online at peopleskitchencollective.com.

00:52:23.290 --> 00:52:28.010
 And you can find us on Instagram and on Facebook and all that good stuff.

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:33.810
 And we do community meals based in the Bay Area but also have been traveling a bit lately.

00:52:34.210 --> 00:52:36.490
 And so, we definitely hope that you can join us at the table.

00:52:36.810 --> 00:52:37.130
 Perfect.

00:52:37.330 --> 00:52:38.790
 Thanks for being here with me today.

00:52:39.050 --> 00:52:39.450
 Thanks.

